WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.320
Do you remember Trello?
You might have used it at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:09.560
an old job, or maybe to plan an event.
It had 50 million users. Over 80% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:15.800
Fortune 500 were using it, and&nbsp;
it was bought for $425 million.&nbsp;

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.520
Trello was everywhere. It defined&nbsp;
an entire style of software.&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.720
But now, Trello's own users are being&nbsp;
told to go use a different product.&nbsp;

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:26.600
Yeah…&nbsp;

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:37.240
So, what happened to Trello?
Looking for the company's next big product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:43.160
Fog Creek CEO Joel Spolsky divided his&nbsp;
software developers into four teams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.440
each free to build whatever they wanted.
Just like any company, the office whiteboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.520
became filled with sticky&nbsp;
notes, tasks, and deadlines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.440
Keeping up with everything was getting harder.
[Michael Pryor, Co-founder]: “If you went into any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:01.080
software-development company, you would see people&nbsp;
would have a whiteboard up, and they would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:07.080
Post-It Notes all over the whiteboard in columns”.
Till one of the teams decided to make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.600
whiteboard itself…the project.
This became Trello.&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.520
Trello is a project management tool built on&nbsp;
the principles of Kanban, a workflow system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:23.640
popular among software developers.
Every task becomes a card that moves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.880
across a board from "To Do"... to&nbsp;
"Doing"... to "Done," giving everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.560
an instant snapshot of what's happening.
Before Trello, teams tracked projects through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.520
endless email chains, spreadsheets,&nbsp;
meeting notes, physical whiteboards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:46.440
or complex Gantt charts. People couldn't easily&nbsp;
see what others were working on or the progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:51.560
they had made, and keeping everyone on the&nbsp;
same page usually meant another meeting.&nbsp;

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.520
Meetings on top of meetings where&nbsp;
people ask “how is this task going?”.&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:02.920
With Trello, you could track multiple projects,&nbsp;
each with its own board. Under each board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.880
you could create different stages of the&nbsp;
project, called lists, which could be named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:14.920
anything. Within each list, you could create cards&nbsp;
that showed what was happening at each stage.&nbsp;

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:20.120
The drag-and-drop feature across the board&nbsp;
made it simple to move cards around even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:27.080
for a project with a large linear workflow.
Today that feels pretty… rudimentary ... but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:33.080
in 2011, this was groundbreaking.&nbsp;
Beats a Gantt chart, that’s for sure.&nbsp;

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:40.600
But this wasn’t the only reason Trello exploded.
The free plan had almost all of its features.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:48.360
Spolsky used his experience as a former program&nbsp;
manager at Excel, yes the Excel, to make the app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:54.440
an alternative for teams outgrowing spreadsheets.
[Joel Spolsky]: “Yeah, it’s that, but it’s also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:59.000
for planning a wedding, for making a list of&nbsp;
potential vacation spots to share with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:05.640
family, for keeping track of applicants to open&nbsp;
job positions, and for a billion other things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:12.040
In fact Trello is for anything where you want to&nbsp;
maintain a list of lists with a group of people.”&nbsp;

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:17.160
But as Trello grew, the simplicity that&nbsp;
made it so successful also came with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.680
big downside, which we’ll get to soon.
The project was funded using bonuses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.520
from Fog Creek employees who believed in&nbsp;
the idea, and eventually... it paid off.&nbsp;

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.480
Trello launched as a finalist&nbsp;
at TechCrunch Disrupt 2011.&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:39.400
Although it didn't win, it attracted&nbsp;
over 131,000 visitors within days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:45.800
and nearly one in four of them signed up.
That’s an insane conversion rate. 22% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.320
visitors joined Trello!
In just six weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:55.400
it had over 100,000 users, and Fog Creek&nbsp;
bootstrapped it for the next three years.&nbsp;

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:03.240
By 2014, it had over 4.75 million users,&nbsp;
and investors soon began to notice.&nbsp;

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.800
They raised $10.3 million in&nbsp;
their first major funding round.&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:16.040
By 2015, Trello had over 10 million users,&nbsp;
with 120,000 new users signing up each week.&nbsp;

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:21.160
But soon, users ran into a bit of a&nbsp;
ceiling. They wanted to do more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:28.040
just move cards around. They wanted more depth.
But here, Trello made one of their first mistakes.&nbsp;

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:33.160
Instead of building those features directly&nbsp;
into Trello, the company introduced Power-Ups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.400
letting other apps fill the gaps.
Solved the problem, for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:42.040
but this would hurt them downstream.
For now, things were booming.&nbsp;

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:49.000
Then came the acquisition. On January&nbsp;
9, 2017, Atlassian bought Trello for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:56.440
$425 million. Everything was going great.
Trello grew from roughly 19 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:04.760
users in 2017 to over 50 million by 2019.
It had made its way into more than 80% of Fortune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:11.240
500 companies. At least, according to Trello…
If everything was going so well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:21.720
how did Trello fall off?
What happened after the Atlassian buyout?&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.960
