00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:08.360
At first, Atlassian had big plans for Trello.&nbsp;
The President, Jay Simons, set a goal of reaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:12.920
100 million monthly active users.
But to do that, Atlassian doubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:18.280
down on a much broader audience.
By then, nearly half of Trello's users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:24.040
came from non-technical roles. But could&nbsp;
one product really meet everyone's needs?&nbsp;

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.640
Trello wanted to build a&nbsp;
product that anyone could use.&nbsp;

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:30.680
[Michael Pryor, Co-founder]: "I think if&nbsp;
you're building a product today, you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:36.920
absolutely 100% start with the idea that the&nbsp;
entire world is going to use your product."&nbsp;

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:42.649
Sounds nice, but, this mindset,&nbsp;
is where the problems began.&nbsp;

00:07:42.649 --> 00:07:46.840
[Joel Spolsky, Fog Creek CEO]: “Making a major&nbsp;
horizontal product that’s useful in any walk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:53.560
life is almost impossible to pull off. You can’t&nbsp;
charge very much, because you’re competing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:58.760
other horizontal products that can amortize&nbsp;
their development costs across a huge number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:05.960
of users. It’s high risk, high reward.”
This was way back in 2012, and he was spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:12.280
on. Unfortunately, Trello struggled to solve&nbsp;
this while newer Kanban boards like Monday.com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:16.760
and Asana filled the gaps.
Its reach was wide, but its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:21.160
capabilities weren't very deep.
Unlike other Kanban boards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:27.000
for simple tasks like time tracking, you had&nbsp;
to integrate another plugin or other apps.&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:33.960
I literally had to do this in a past job. We&nbsp;
used Trello, and a tracking app called Hubstaff.&nbsp;

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:40.440
Meanwhile, Monday.com and Asana had built-in&nbsp;
time tracking, centralized communication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:45.960
and calendar views without needing users&nbsp;
to leave the app. Great for businesses who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:51.720
want an all-in-one, plug-and-play solution.
I've used Trello in a few different jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:56.440
and it felt great to use, but&nbsp;
it never felt overly robust.&nbsp;

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.200
Companies seemed to start&nbsp;
with it, then outgrow it.&nbsp;

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:05.800
Trello catered to everyday users and business&nbsp;
professionals, but those audiences played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:11.880
very different roles in its business model.&nbsp;
This was the beginning of several mistakes.&nbsp;

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:19.000
First, it anchored its business around the free&nbsp;
plan. The app was entirely free until 2013, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:26.840
it introduced customizable board backgrounds, 250&nbsp;
MB of attachment storage per board, and stickers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:34.040
and emojis as reasons to upgrade to a paid plan.
Well... not many bought into that.&nbsp;

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:40.840
That same year, it also introduced Business&nbsp;
Class for $200 a year per organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:44.600
That's absurdly generous.
Some SaaS companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:50.040
charge that per seat, per month.
Meanwhile, Trello Business offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:57.080
unlimited integrations, additional collaboration&nbsp;
features, read-only views, and privacy settings.&nbsp;

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:03.080
But none of these changed what Trello actually&nbsp;
did. The free plan already handled the core job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:08.760
of managing projects, so many businesses&nbsp;
saw little reason to pay for extras.&nbsp;

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:12.120
And when it was time to throw&nbsp;
serious money around, other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:18.360
products offered deeper, and better solutions.
Around the time Atlassian acquired the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:23.720
Trello had around 19 million registered&nbsp;
users but was generating only about $10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:30.600
million a year in revenue. That's roughly 50&nbsp;
cents per registered user… per year, meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:37.000
only a tiny percentage of users were paying for&nbsp;
everyone else, and a minuscule amount at that.&nbsp;

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:42.280
Trello had millions of users, but far&nbsp;
too few of them were paying customers.&nbsp;

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:48.120
There was a bit of tension. Make a simple&nbsp;
platform that can be used by everyone? Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:55.480
a deeper software for those who’ll pay us?
…Was it too late to make Trello profitable?&nbsp;

00:10:55.480 --> 00:11:02.040
Atlassian realized it needed to change something.
For years, Trello had let teams create virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:10.040
unlimited boards for free, charging only for&nbsp;
extra features. But in 2019, that changed. Free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:16.280
teams were now limited to just ten boards.&nbsp;
A year later, Trello introduced Workspaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:23.000
and every board had to belong to one, making&nbsp;
that ten-board limit much harder to avoid.&nbsp;

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:28.280
Atlassian were trying to monetize Trello,&nbsp;
but the problem is people are more willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:33.000
to pay for new features, than pay for&nbsp;
features that were free in the past.&nbsp;

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.960
This is called loss aversion. We&nbsp;
don’t tend to like losing things.&nbsp;

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:43.400
Users weren’t too thrilled.
And, all of this gets even weirder.&nbsp;

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:50.440
It turns out, all this time, Atlassian had an&nbsp;
entirely separate Kanban product. In reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.440 --> 00:12:00.600
Atlassian wasn't really buying Trello,&nbsp;
in the acquisition, but something else.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:04.600
If you’ve watched this far, consider&nbsp;
subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:11.080
and means you won’t miss future videos.
With time, the $425 million acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:18.920
of a $10 million business started to make sense.&nbsp;
Atlassian wanted the users more than the business.&nbsp;

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:26.440
At roughly $22 per registered user, it was buying&nbsp;
millions of potential customers. It was a customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:31.400
acquisition… acquisition. Hah.
So why do this?&nbsp;

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:36.600
Well, we need to talk about&nbsp;
Atlassian’s other Kanban product: Jira.&nbsp;

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:41.560
It was one of the most powerful project&nbsp;
management tools for software teams.&nbsp;

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:48.120
Very customized and deep. And like other deep SaaS&nbsp;
tools, once a company built its workflow around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:55.160
Jira, it became incredibly difficult to leave.
Trello served a completely different audience.&nbsp;

00:12:55.160 --> 00:13:02.040
The app’s simplicity made Trello easy to adopt...&nbsp;
and that was exactly what Atlassian wanted.&nbsp;

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:06.200
They wanted to funnel people&nbsp;
from Trello… into Jira.&nbsp;

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:12.600
But there was a problem. Trello still had&nbsp;
some business use, even if it was simple.&nbsp;

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:20.280
That meant it was overlapping with Jira. There&nbsp;
was a real risk of self-cannibalization. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:25.720
Trello still wasn’t making real money.
And things were getting even worse.&nbsp;

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:31.800
As the Kanban style became more popular,&nbsp;
competitors caught up. Being first was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:38.200
no longer enough. Monday.com and Asana&nbsp;
built their products around teams, making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:46.040
themselves harder to replace as companies grew.
There’s also many more, like Notion and ClickUp.&nbsp;

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:50.760
Trello, on the other hand, barely&nbsp;
changed. For nearly three years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:58.120
Atlassian introduced almost no major new features.&nbsp;
It just, sort of got more expensive to use.&nbsp;

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:02.120
Then, Atlassian began the&nbsp;
next stage of their plan.&nbsp;

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:08.600
In 2025, an Atlassian team member, when responding&nbsp;
to users asking for more robust features,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:14.440
said “You can also consider Jira for&nbsp;
more sophisticated team collaboration”.&nbsp;

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:17.480
Then again, in the same thread, “Trello&nbsp;
is changing and turning towards personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:24.040
productivity, and we continue to recommend Jira&nbsp;
for team collaboration and project management”.&nbsp;

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:29.960
Apparently Trello started displaying a huge&nbsp;
banner encouraging users to move to Jira. Though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.640
I dunno if this was true.
And it got worse.&nbsp;

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:42.040
On May 22, 2025, Trello overhauled almost its&nbsp;
entire interface. The layout and fonts changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:47.640
Even the buttons worked differently. Features&nbsp;
people had relied on for years, like adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:52.840
comments to cards, suddenly disappeared.
Trello was becoming shallower.&nbsp;

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:58.360
The backlash was immediate.
“Is Trello dead? Anymore updates or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:04.200
enhancements? Change layout of boards? Should&nbsp;
I start looking for something new and start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:09.320
moving my work and personal life over?”
“Change isn't always a good thing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:14.200
change for changes sake definitely isn't&nbsp;
recommended...The lack of change with Trello&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:19.160
from 2020 until 2024 has been coupled&nbsp;
with us seeing Atlassian do everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:25.240
in their power to shift Trello users to Jira”
An open letter to Trello's leadership appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:31.080
on Reddit, with users comparing the redesign&nbsp;
to Windows 8. Yeah you know it’s bad when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:35.800
Windows 8 is the comparison.
But… nothing came of it.&nbsp;

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:40.600
It’s clear what Atlassian was doing:&nbsp;
Pushing Trello back to personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:46.680
projects… and ONLY for personal use.
Users noticed that Jira's cheapest paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:54.440
tier costs $7.53 against Trello's $5.00, and&nbsp;
speculated Atlassian was pushing professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:58.920
users up the price ladder.
Users were, justifiably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:05.320
rather annoyed. Unfortunately, Trello&nbsp;
existed in a weird spot between markets.&nbsp;

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:10.840
Then Atlassian hammered in the last nail.
“Going forward, we will not be taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:16.680
new feature requests related to project&nbsp;
management. Instead, our work will prioritize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:22.920
use cases related to personal productivity.”
Of course, the Trello support agent encourages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:28.440
people to move to Jira. Surprise surprise.
In the end, Trello's biggest strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:32.760
became its biggest weakness.
It built a product so simple that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:39.560
almost anyone could use it... but it never really&nbsp;
figured out how to chase upmarket customers.&nbsp;

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:45.240
Every attempt to fix that slowly chipped away&nbsp;
at the very thing that made Trello special.&nbsp;

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:51.160
Today, Trello holds less than 2% of the&nbsp;
productivity market it helped pioneer.&nbsp;

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:58.280
Ironically, Atlassian is doing better than ever,&nbsp;
generating more than $6 billion in annual revenue.&nbsp;

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:05.320
Trello had finally found its place. Just, at&nbsp;
the expense of those who originally loved it.

00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:10.280
Ironically, one of the SaaS tools that&nbsp;
replaced Trello, is now dying. You might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:16.840
have seen this brand: Monday.com. It was&nbsp;
basically advertised on every YouTube video.&nbsp;

00:17:16.840 --> 00:17:21.400
But now, it’s collapsing,&nbsp;
and for a surprising reason.

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:23.720
